<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sihor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sihor is a town in the Bhavnagar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, west of Bhavnagar city. From the 13th century it was a seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gohil">Gohil Rajputs</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhavsinhji-i">Bhavsinhji I</a> moved his capital to the new port of Bhavnagar in 1723. It retains an old palace of the Gohils and is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and brass work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sihor.jpg" alt="Sihor" width="300" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shivnag · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cross_Section_of_one_of_Sihor_Hill_Ranges,_from_Sukhnath.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sihor lies on the river Gautami, surrounded by hills, about 20 km from Bhavnagar, at an average elevation of 60 metres. According to Hasmukh Dhirajlal Sankalia its name comes from Siṁhapura, attested in inscriptions of the Maitraka and Chaulukya eras, after either the lion, native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, or a man named Siṁha; over time the root became sīha and the suffix -pura was shortened to -or. Other forms of the name recorded are Saraswatpur, Sinhalpur, Sinhpur, Sinhor and Shihor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sihor was a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gohil">Gohil Rajputs</a>, and its many temples have earned it the local name Chhote Kashi, a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. Its monuments include the 12th-century Brahma Kund, a stepped tank surrounded by images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deities and attributed to Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-siddharaja">Jayasimha Siddharaja</a>, the 17th-century Vijay Vilas Palace of the maharajas with its paintings and wood carvings, the old fort and town walls, and the nine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temples of the Navnath pilgrimage, built or renewed between about 1000 and 1600 and visited above all in the month of Shraavan.</p><p>Local tradition links the town with two figures. One makes it the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Prince Vijaya</a>, remembered as the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, who is said to have sailed from nearby Ghogha; other traditions place his origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Another holds that Nana Sahib, a leader of the rising of 1857, lived out his life in Sihor and died there in 1909; a house in the old town, a tomb and a park are associated with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sihor is a town and municipality in Bhavnagar district with a population of about 26,000 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It became a railway junction when the Bhavnagar State Railway reached it in 1880, and it is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and brass ware, pottery, snuff making and rolling mills.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sihor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Gohil Rajputs had ruled in southern Saurashtra since the 13th century from <b>Sihor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhavnagar-state">Bhavnagar State</a></li></ul></section>
